\pard\pardeftab720\ri380\sb200

\b \cf0 Kuala Lumpur
\b0 \
\cf5 In the mid 1990s, Malaysia was undergoing a huge change. Dr Mahathir Mohamad, Malaysia's Prime Minister was determined that by 2020, the country would be a fully industrialised nation. The best way to do this, it was thought, was through the auto trade.\uc0\u8232 \u8232 The nation's number one oil company, Petronas, began investing heavily in Formula One with Sauber, and Malaysia's national car company, Proton, bought Lotus Engineering.\
The most extravagant part of the plan however was the building of a multimedia 'supercorridor', linking Kuala Lumpur's new international airport with the capital city itself.\
Designed by Hermann Tilke, Sepang is one of the most technical circuits in Formula One. The combination of long high-speed straights, and tight twisting complexes make the track very complicated, but also perfect for overtaking as the track itself is very wide.\cf0 \

\b Why go? 
\b0 \
\cf5 Malaysia offers a fascinating mix of urban sophistication and rural charm. The cities - and particularly the capital Kuala Lumpur (KL) - are developing extremely quickly, but the countryside has maintained its innocence. The people continue to farm the land and the sea, as they have for centuries.\'a0\
\'93Malaysia is a fantastic place,\'94 says FIA President\'a0Jean Todt. \'93I love spicy food, so when I'm in Kuala Lumpur, I go and visit one of the local restaurants in Petaling Street or Chinatown. I also like to have massages in one of the traditional spas, or go shopping near the Twin Towers.\'94\

\b Did you know?
\b0 \
At 452 metres, Petronas Towers in the centre of Kuala Lumpur remains the world\'92s tallest twin-towered public building.\
